WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:18.924
Laurie carried Amy all the way home, talking to her, trying to keep her from falling asleep, because that would have lowered her body temperature even more.

2
00:00:18.925 --> 00:00:25.272
Jo was trying her best to contribute to the rescue mission but her whole body and mind were numb.

3
00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:28.293
"What if she dies? It'll be my fault.

4
00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:34.571
How can I live with myself if Amy dies?" Jo repeated over and over again under her breath.

5
00:00:34.572 --> 00:00:39.567
"She's not going to die, Jo. Just keep talking to her," Laurie panted.

6
00:00:39.568 --> 00:00:50.049
Even though his main concern was Amy, Laurie's thoughts never left his best friend, who he knew felt guilty about the unfortunate accident.

7
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.779
"Mother March!" Laurie called out, kicking the door open with his foot when they got to the March home.

8
00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.153
Laurie was the only one who was allowed to call Mrs. March by that name.

9
00:01:00.154 --> 00:01:05.758
"Heavens! What's happened?" Mother gasped as soon as she saw the three of them burst into the house.

10
00:01:05.759 --> 00:01:10.979
Laying Amy in the chair closest to the fireplace, Laurie tried his best to explain.

11
00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:20.840
For some reason, seeing her Mother's pale-white face made Jo feel even guiltier and she could not control the tears that started to slide down her cheeks.

12
00:01:20.841 --> 00:01:23.965
Meanwhile, Mother was giving instructions to everyone.

13
00:01:23.966 --> 00:01:29.486
"Hannah! We need more wood. Meg, get some hot towels and boil a pot of hot water.

14
00:01:29.487 --> 00:01:33.934
Beth, go upstairs and grab every blanket you can find and bring them quickly.

15
00:01:33.935 --> 00:01:36.315
We need to keep her as warm as possible."

16
00:01:36.316 --> 00:01:40.280
Laurie stepped back, letting Mother take charge of the situation.

17
00:01:40.281 --> 00:01:44.260
He shivered, not realizing until then how cold he was.

18
00:01:44.261 --> 00:01:51.902
Warming himself by the fire, he glanced over at Jo, who was shaking visibly from the cold, but did not seem aware of it.

19
00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:57.377
Wordlessly, he went over to her, putting his arms around her shoulders and patting her gently.

20
00:01:57.378 --> 00:02:04.499
Jo, grateful for Laurie's kindness, leaned her head against him, and sobbed until no more tears would come out.

21
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:10.198
With the exception of a slight numbness in her hands and feet, Amy was going to be fine.

22
00:02:10.199 --> 00:02:16.048
Laurie and Jo had acted speedily and saved Amy before any severe damage had happened.

23
00:02:16.049 --> 00:02:21.294
Still, Jo could not help but feel that Amy's fall was her fault in the first place.

24
00:02:21.295 --> 00:02:27.528
"Jo? Come here, dearest," Mother whispered when she saw Jo standing awkwardly at the doorway.

25
00:02:27.529 --> 00:02:32.397
Mother had just come in to check up on Amy, who was sleeping soundly.

26
00:02:32.398 --> 00:02:36.367
Jo paced outside the bedroom but did not have the courage to go inside.

27
00:02:36.368 --> 00:02:40.104
Her mother's kind words, though, seemed a warm invitation.

28
00:02:40.105 --> 00:02:47.362
"Amy is doing fine. She just needs to rest," Mother spoke softly. "You need some rest too."

29
00:02:47.363 --> 00:02:52.024
Jo felt her throat tighten and the tears sting her eyes once again.

30
00:02:52.025 --> 00:02:58.963
"Jo, whatever you are feeling, this was not your fault," Mother said, sensing Jo's painful guilt.

31
00:02:58.964 --> 00:03:08.812
"But it was. If I hadn't been so mean, she . . . if only I hadn't lost my temper . . ." Jo choked.

32
00:03:08.813 --> 00:03:13.500
Mother pulled Jo close to her and whispered, "I understand how you feel."

33
00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:18.261
"How can you know what it feels like to be so angry, Mother?" sniffed Jo.

34
00:03:18.262 --> 00:03:22.007
"You're the most patient, kindest person in the world."

35
00:03:22.008 --> 00:03:30.506
Mother smiled shyly and said, "I try very hard but, to be honest, I used to be exactly like you."

36
00:03:30.507 --> 00:03:36.736
Jo stared at her mother unbelievingly. Mother was a role model for all her daughters.

37
00:03:36.737 --> 00:03:41.951
For her to admit that she had a temper like Jo's was too much of a shock.

38
00:03:41.952 --> 00:03:49.137
Shaking her head, Jo said, "No, I can't believe that, Mother. You're not like me at all."

39
00:03:49.138 --> 00:03:57.666
Chuckling quietly, Mother replied, "You may think I am perfect and make no mistakes but Jo, I'm also human.

40
00:03:57.667 --> 00:04:04.073
I have many faults and weaknesses. I'm just better able to control them as I get older."

41
00:04:04.074 --> 00:04:09.115
Jo, no longer crying, quietly contemplated her mother's words.

42
00:04:09.116 --> 00:04:13.085
"Do you think I will ever change and be like you?"

43
00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:16.362
"That depends on whether or not you want to change.

44
00:04:16.363 --> 00:04:20.676
Some people feel that anger makes them stronger and more respected.

45
00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:25.691
Others see that anger, though sometimes unavoidable, can be controlled.

46
00:04:25.692 --> 00:04:28.635
That is what I am learning," said Mother.

47
00:04:28.636 --> 00:04:32.209
Amy stirred and mumbled something softly in her sleep.

48
00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:40.901
Watching her, Jo confessed, "I thought I could never forgive her for destroying my story. I didn't want to forgive her."

49
00:04:40.902 --> 00:04:44.008
"How do you feel now?" Mother asked kindly.

50
00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:47.644
"I realize that I can always write another story.

51
00:04:47.645 --> 00:04:55.823
Amy might have ruined what I wrote down, but I still have it preserved in here." Jo tapped her heart.

52
00:04:55.824 --> 00:05:04.495
"And," she continued, "although stories may be replaceable, if I lost Amy, there would be no one to take her place."

53
00:05:04.496 --> 00:05:11.600
Mother gave Jo a kiss and patted her hand, saying, "Yes, you can and will change, Jo.

54
00:05:11.601 --> 00:05:15.507
One day, that temper of yours will no longer control you."

55
00:05:15.508 --> 00:05:19.654
Mother got up and left Jo to sit with Amy for a while.

56
00:05:19.655 --> 00:05:24.833
Jo started to feel better, knowing that perhaps she wasn't so hopeless.

57
00:05:24.834 --> 00:05:31.964
"If Mother can change, so can I. I am her daughter, after all," she said to herself.

58
00:05:31.965 --> 00:05:36.897
"Jo?" Amy whispered weakly, her eyes half-opened.

59
00:05:36.898 --> 00:05:40.908
"I'm here, darling," Jo replied warmly.

60
00:05:40.909 --> 00:05:44.107
Amy stared at her for a long time before speaking.

61
00:05:44.108 --> 00:05:49.103
"Jo? I'm so sorry. Please forgive me."

62
00:05:49.104 --> 00:05:57.274
Wiping her hand across her eyes, Jo, who had never cried so much in her life, took Amy's hand and held it close to her face.

63
00:05:57.275 --> 00:06:04.553
"Me, too, Amy. Let's forget all that's happened in the past and look forward. What do you say?"

64
00:06:04.554 --> 00:06:11.189
Amy gave Jo a faint smile and squeezed her hand as she answered, "Thank you, Jo."

65
00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:17.955
That evening, Jo could not sleep. Her mind was racing with thoughts about the events of the past few days.

66
00:06:17.956 --> 00:06:24.326
She suddenly found herself wanting to put everything down in writing because a story was forming in her head.

67
00:06:24.327 --> 00:06:28.872
When the other girls had gone to sleep, Jo crept up to her sanctuary,

68
00:06:28.873 --> 00:06:35.170
a place she had not visited since Amy had destroyed her story, and sat at the old familiar desk.

69
00:06:35.171 --> 00:06:42.358
She began to write, her pen flying over the paper, while she tried to jot down every word spilling out of her.

70
00:06:42.359 --> 00:06:45.953
In the end, Jo stayed up all night completing her story.

71
00:06:45.954 --> 00:06:53.814
Finally, Jo leaned back in her chair and, stretching her neck and shoulders, thought of the perfect title for her story.

72
00:06:53.815 --> 00:07:01.752
Taking a clean sheet of paper, she wrote in big letters "Like Mother, Like Daughter—A story by Josephine March."

73
00:07:01.753 --> 00:07:12.150
Cradling her chin in her hand, Jo watched the sun rise, glad to be alive and to enjoy another beautiful day.
